  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"terminal#3454\", \"terminal#9218\", \"terminal#18937\"], \"answer\": \"Terminal has had this request before, and it isn't implemented as a Terminal setting. There is a workaround for the always-visible part. Turn off Windows Settings > Accessibility (Ease of Access) > Visual effects > 'Automatically hide scroll bars in Windows'. Terminal follows that system-wide setting. Since Terminal Preview 1.17 (#14047), the change applies to open windows right away. Scrollbars then stay visible and expanded, so you don't have to hover to bring them up. Terminal also has a `scrollbarState: \\\"visible\\\"` setting. A custom width is a different matter. #9218 asked for a width setting. The team found the XAML scrollbar width hard to change, and later said they didn't want per-app scrollbar settings. They said this should be handled at the OS or WinUI level. #18937 asked for the same width option and was closed without a change. The width part is probably not going to be added in Terminal. Your best option is to raise it with Windows through Feedback Hub or the WinUI repo, as a system-wide accessibility request. You can also upvote or comment on #3454 and #9218.\"}",
